# Data & Splits

## Always understand the data first

- Visualize distributions for F2 docking scores before modeling; note oddities (clumps, outliers, missingness, score scale)
- If values look wrong, investigate before training
- Featurize with Morgan fingerprints and/or RDKit / Descriptastorus descriptors; report which features look important for F2

## SMILES hygiene (required)

- Standardize and neutralize SMILES with RDKit before hashing, splitting, or training
- De-duplicate on standardized SMILES
- Keep a small reusable helper for standardize → neutralize → canonical SMILES

## Never random-split chemical data

- Use **scaffold** and/or **chemical similarity** splits so test molecules are not near-duplicates of train
- Preferred for this project: **ChemProp-style similarity split** with **Morgan fingerprints**, **Tanimoto threshold 0.7**
- When reporting metrics, state the split method and threshold explicitly

```python
# ❌ BAD — random split leaks near-neighbors across folds
train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)

# ✅ GOOD — scaffold or similarity split (threshold ~0.7 Morgan/Tanimoto)
# document split type + fingerprint radius/bits + threshold in the notebook/script
```
